Result is here. (test code is call imread("Output.tif") only ).

```bash
$ ./a.out
tile_width0 = 32768
tile_height0 = 5462
ncn = 3
(int)(bpp / bitsPerByte) = 2
TIFF tile size = 1073872896
buffer_size = 715915264
```

In this case. 48bit RGB Image is converted to 32bit RGBA Image in `TIFFReadRGBAStrip()` via libtiff.  So `buffer_size` is smaller than `TIFF tile size`  about -25%. 

>                // we will use TIFFReadRGBA* functions, so allocate temporary buffer for 32bit RGBA
>                bpp = 8;
>                ncn = 4;

Like this phenomenon, 24bit RGB Image is converted to 32bit RGBA Image too. So `buffer_size` is larger than `TIFF tile size`  about +33%.

```
$ ./a.out
tile_width0 = 32768
tile_height0 = 10924
ncn = 3
(int)(bpp / bitsPerByte) = 1
TIFF tile size = 1073872896
buffer_size = 1431830528
```

So I believe this CV_Assert() is not good to prevent over memory allocation completely.

## How to fix ( temporary )

I think moving CV_Assert() is better.

```c++
            const uint64_t MAX_TILE_SIZE = (CV_BIG_UINT(1) << 30);
            CV_CheckLE((int)ncn, 4, "");
            CV_CheckLE((int)bpp, 64, "");
-           CV_Assert(((uint64_t)tile_width0 * tile_height0 * ncn * std::max(1, (int)(bpp / bitsPerByte)) < MAX_TILE_SIZE) && "TIFF tile size is too large: >= 1Gb");

            const size_t buffer_size = (bpp / bitsPerByte) * ncn * tile_height0 * tile_width0;
+            CV_Assert( buffer_size < MAX_TILE_SIZE) && "buffer_size is too large: >= 1Gb");
            AutoBuffer<uchar> _buffer(buffer_size);
            uchar* buffer = _buffer.data();
            ushort* buffer16 = (ushort*)buffer;

```

